### Unable to save RStudio Preferences

[Example issue](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/data-platform-support/issues/1427)

Some users encounter an issue where they cannot save their preferences in RStudio as the preferences file is not owned by their user. This bug seems to have been introduced in RStudio 4.5.1 release, and has been fixed in RStudio 4.5.1-1.
Ask users to try upgrading to 4.5.1-1 in the first instance.

If this does not work, you can resolve manually using the steps below:

- Access EFS in the prod cluster - kubectl exec -i -t -n default efs-app -c app "--" sh
- Navigate to the user's Rstudio config folder - cd /data/<user>/rstudio/.config/rstudio
- Change the ownership of the preferences file to the user - chown 1000:1000 rstudio-prefs.json
- Ask the user to try and save their preferences.

### Unable to sign on to Analytical Platform Control Panel

Users may not be able to log into the control panel for a variety of reasons. Some common reasons for this may be:

- They may be using the email box rather than using the GitHub button when prompted to login. The email box is for CICA users only - [Example issue](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/data-platform-support/issues/1451)
    - Ask the user to try using the Continue with GitHub button.

- They may not be part of the moj-analytical-services GitHub org - [Example issue](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/data-platform-support/issues/1446)
    - Check the above analytical services link and search for the user.
    - If they aren't part of the org, accessing it is self-serve if the user has a justice email.

- A very rare instance may involve errors in sentry that look like [this error example](https://ministryofjustice.sentry.io/issues/6499497310/?environment=prod&project=5606313&query=is%3Aunresolved&referrer=issue-stream) or [this error example](https://ministryofjustice.sentry.io/issues/6499497310/?environment=prod&project=5606313&query=is%3Aunresolved&referrer=issue-stream)
    - This appears to be the result of a new user trying to access control panel in the middle of an AWS outage
    - Check the status of their provision user chart - helm ls --all --namespace <user-username>. The likelihood will be it has failed
    - Before clearing check in EFS to see if a directory with their username exists by running the following commands:

        ```sh
        kubectl exec -i -t -n default efs-app -c app "--" sh
        cd /data
        ls -la
        ```
        Look for their user in the list (might be a better way of finding their folder).
        If the folder exists, delete it.

    - Clear their provision user chart - helm uninstall provision-user-<username> --namespace user-<username>
    - Ask the user to log back into control panel

- If neither of these are the issue, investigate using both [Sentry](https://ministryofjustice.sentry.io/issues/?environment=prod&project=5606313&statsPeriod=30d) and [Auth0](https://manage.auth0.com/dashboard/eu/alpha-analytics-moj/).
- Either of these may output useful logging when a user is failing to log in.

### Higher Memory or GPU Version of Tooling

[Example issue](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/data-platform-support/issues/1444)
Users may request a high memory or GPU version of whatever tool they primarily use.

To resolve:

- Go to the [Tool Releases](https://controlpanel.services.analytical-platform.service.justice.gov.uk/releases/) page on the control panel.
- If you cannot access this, you will need Superuser permissions. Ask someone to grant you this.
- Use the filters to look for restricted releases of a given tool.
- Click the Manage Release button of the tool you wish to grant access to.
- High memory versions are typically labelled 2vCPU, 24GB RAM or something similar.
- GPU versions are labelled with GPU
- Add the user's github username to the 'Target Users' box and click the Update Tool Release button.
- The user list for a restricted tool is comma separated.

### Add a reader to the legacy Quicksight

[Example issue](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/data-platform-support/issues/1442)
We may receive requests to add users to an older version of QuickSight.

To resolve:

- Go to the AWS console for the analytical-platform-data-production account.
- Go to QuickSight.
- Click on the person icon in the top right of the UI and select Manage QuickSight.
- Click the invite users button.
- Type the email address of the user to add in the box and hit enter.
- Select Reader under Role and then click the invite button.
- This will send an invite email.

Note:  QuickSight treats email addresses (used as usernames) as case-sensitive
If the user was invited as `Fred.Blogs@email.address.com` but attempted to sign in as `fred.blogs@email.address.com`,
they will have issues.

### Add an author to the legacy Quicksight

We generally do not want to onboard new users to the legacy Quicksight, in particular new authors. Exceptions are made for teams that are already using it, and need to onboard new team members.
The process is different to adding readers (as detailed above):

1. Log in to Control Panel as a superuser
2. Click "List all users", find the user and load their profile. If they do not exist, they need to follow user guidance to [get set up in Control Panel](https://user-guidance.analytical-platform.service.justice.gov.uk/get-started.html#4-access-the-analytical-platform).
3. Under "QuickSight access" check "Legacy QuickSight access enabled (running in the data-production account)" and save
4. Ask the user to log in to QuickSight, via the AWS console, following these steps:
    1. Log in to Control Panel
    2. Go to Your Tools
    3. Click the "access AWS services such as S3 and Athena via the AWS Console" link to open the AWS console
    4. Search for Quicksight
    5. On initial login, the user will have been created as an author

IMPORTANT: if the user also requires Athena access (for example, if they need to create a Datasource from Athena), this needs to granted from within Quicksight:

1. Access the data-production AWS account as an admin
2. Access Quicksight from the AWS console
3. Within Quicksight, use the dropdown in the top right corner of the page to access the "Manage account" page.
4. On the left hand side, scroll down to Permissions, and click "IAM policy Assignments"
5. Find the "AthenaQSAccess" assignment, scroll to the right to find the "Action" column, and click the edit icon
6. Use the "Assign users and groups" step to add a user. Searching by the users email address (as stored in the Control Panel) works best.

Note: I could not launch "IAM policy Assignments" without been asked to sign in again until I enabled AWS multi session feature.

### The moj-analytical-services organisation has enabled or enforced SAML SSO

[Example issue](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/data-platform-support/issues/1420)
Users may raise this when trying and failing to clone repositories to their tooling. This typically signals that a user hasn't created an SSH key within their tooling.

To resolve:

- Direct them to the user guidance Quickstart guide for [Jupyter](https://user-guidance.analytical-platform.service.justice.gov.uk/get-started.html#5-set-up-jupyterlab), [RStudio](https://user-guidance.analytical-platform.service.justice.gov.uk/get-started.html#6-set-up-rstudio) or [VSCode](https://user-guidance.analytical-platform.service.justice.gov.uk/get-started.html#7-set-up-vscode).

### Tooling fails to deploy

Users' tooling may fail to deploy via control panel. This can happen for several reasons.

- It could relate to helm failing to deploy the tool. This may be surfaced in Sentry as a ToolDeploymentError with the description HelmError('Error: UPGRADE FAILED: another operation (install/upgrade/rollback) is in progress\n') - [Example issue](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/data-platform-support/issues/1373)
    - To fix this, you will need to clear the offending helm deploy.
    - In the prod cluster run helm ls --all --namespace <user-username>. This will get a list of all helm deploys for a user. The status will be something other than deployed for the failed deploy. Get the chart name from here.
    - Run `helm uninstall <chart-name> --namespace <user-username>`. This will remove the chart and the tool deployment.
    - Ask the user to reinstall their tool.

- The user may have changed their GitHub username - [Example issue](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/data-platform-support/issues/1417)
    - When launching the tool, it will use their old GitHub username as part of the URL. Check the username at the start of URL and see if it matches against the username that has submitted the support request.
    - If they don't match, the user will have to start fresh. This will involve deleting their old user from control panel and the user logging in to create a new user.
    - To delete a user:
        - Go to the [users page in the control panel admin](https://controlpanel.services.analytical-platform.service.justice.gov.uk/users/).
        - Find the old user and click Manage User.
        - Click the Delete User button.
        - Click OK on the alert box that pops up.

